---
import "@skryensya/core/components/steps.css";
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { stepsTree, stepsVerticalTree } from "../../../demos/steps";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

---

<Base title="Steps" description="Steps: linear progress indicator with complete, current, and upcoming states.">
  <h1>Steps</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Steps shows progress in an ordered sequence. Each step reports one of three states <code>complete</code>, <code>current</code>, <code>upcoming</code>; the component displays the sequence, it does not govern which is active or save navigation between steps.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={stepsTree(t)} label="Steps" />
  <p>In React, <code>current</code> derives state from steps that do not declare theirs; one step can overwrite it with <code>status</code>. <code>description</code> adds context without converting the step to a card.</p>

  <h2>On mobile</h2>
  <p>
    When the viewport does not reach <code>40rem</code>, the path becomes a vertical rail
    automatically: the marker is on the left and each label retains its full width. There is no
    truncation or horizontal scrolling.
  </p>

  <h2>Option: vertical at any size</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-orientation="vertical"</code> requests the same rail at any viewport width, not just
    on mobile: the use case is a wizard with content to the right, where the step-by-step is the
    navigation of a fixed sidebar instead of a narrow-space fallback. The mark
    <code>data-orientation="horizontal"</code> does the reverse: it forces the horizontal path even
    below <code>40rem</code>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={stepsVerticalTree(t)} label="Vertical Steps" />

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
</Base>
